          "text": "{\"relation\":\"MATCH\",\"consensus_summary\":\"Liberty is spiritual freedom in Christ from bondage to sin, death, condemnation, and the law as a means of justification, ordered toward holiness, love, righteousness, and service rather than license.\",\"book_summary\":\"Liberty is the King’s jubilee release from slavery to sin into God’s household, inheritance, and faithful service—a transfer of masters rather than autonomy from authority or moral obligation.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Christ-given release from slavery to sin into righteous service.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"The King’s release from slavery to sin into faithful service under God.\",\"core_relation\":\"EQUIVALENT\",\"extension_relation\":\"SAME_CASES\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"No material boundary disagreement. The book’s denial that liberty abolishes the law’s obligations is compatible with the consensus distinction between freedom from the law’s condemnation or use as a means of justification and freedom for obedient holiness.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"None. Both readings reject liberty as license and describe it as liberation from sin that results in service to God rather than unrestricted autonomy.\",\"rationale\":\"Reduced to their minimum identifications, both accounts define liberty as divinely granted freedom from bondage to sin that places the liberated person in righteous service to God. The book’s jubilee, household, inheritance, kingship, and change-of-masters language develops the same identification without replacing it or materially changing which cases count as liberty.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"The printed texts support jubilee release, captivity, the law of liberty, and the contrast between liberty and slavery to corruption, but they do not by themselves explicitly establish every element of the entry—especially the King’s agency, entry into God’s household and inheritance, or the precise formulation that liberty changes masters.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
